# Ghostty cheatsheet

## Fixing `'xterm-ghostty': unknown terminal type` over SSH

Original from: https://gist.github.com/nseba/9d96bca064ff1ae06cf21ae26e1bc7a2/

When connecting to a remote host with [Ghostty](https://ghostty.org), commands like
`clear`, `tmux`, or `less` may fail with:

```
'xterm-ghostty': unknown terminal type.
```

This happens because the remote host doesn't have Ghostty's `terminfo` entry. Here are
three fixes, easiest first.

### 1. Quick fix (per command)

Override `TERM` for a single command:

```sh
TERM=xterm-256color clear
```

### 2. Set `TERM` for the SSH session

In your local `~/.ssh/config`:

```
Host myserver
    SetEnv TERM=xterm-256color
```

> Requires `AcceptEnv TERM` on the server (often the default). If it's not set, this
> silently has no effect.

### 3. Proper fix — install Ghostty's terminfo on the remote

Export your local `xterm-ghostty` terminfo and install it into the remote user's
`~/.terminfo`:

```sh
infocmp -x | ssh myserver -- tic -x -
```

This is the recommended option: it preserves Ghostty's full terminal capabilities
instead of downgrading `TERM`. Options 1 and 2 are fine when you just need it working
right now.